The usual Einstein equation,
\begin{equation}
G_{\mu\nu} = \frac{8\pi G}{c^4} T_{\mu\nu},
\end{equation}
relates curvature ($G_{\mu\nu}$) to stress–energy ($T_{\mu\nu}$). MK‑43 Ultra extends the relation by asking what happens if part of the curvature is \emph{never} allowed to land in $T_{\mu\nu}$ at all, because it lives in a deeper combinatorial layer---a quantum geometry sea that can curve, resonate, and store information without ever showing up as ordinary mass or radiation.

That silent sea is what cosmology nicknamed ``dark matter''. MK‑43 Ultra calls it what it really is: \emph{the load‑bearing geometry of reality}.

Combining the Einstein--Hilbert term with phase, chromatic, and ER contributions, we write
\begin{equation}
  S_{\mathrm{tot}} =
  \frac{1}{16\pi G}\int d^4x \sqrt{-g}\, R
  + S_\tau + S_\chi + S_{ER} + S_{\mathrm{matter}},
\end{equation}
where $S_{ER}$ is an effective ER network action and $S_{\mathrm{matter}}$ collects any additional fields.

\section{Field equations}

Variation with respect to the metric yields
\begin{equation}
  G_{\mu\nu}
  =
  8\pi G\, \left(
    T^{(\tau)}_{\mu\nu}
    + T^{(\chi)}_{\mu\nu}
    + T^{(ER)}_{\mu\nu}
    + T^{\mathrm{(matter)}}_{\mu\nu}
  \right).
\end{equation}

One of the central claims of MK--43 Ultra is that the apparent attractive nature of gravity arises geometrically from the way phase gradients, chromatic energy densities, and ER connectivity jointly curve the metric so as to favour configurations in which energy aggregates rather than disperses.

